lion to Nez's $3.5 million and reserved $2.1 million for fall advertising. Most independent raters place the race in the Likely Republican column, though a few call it Lean Republican and one calls it a tossup.\n\nPolling has been sparse and mixed. A Democratic-sponsored August survey showed Crane ahead by only two points, while a Republican-sponsored poll around the same time showed an eleven-point lead. Prediction markets give Crane roughly a two-in-three chance of winning. Nez has campaigned on water infrastructure and cost-of-living issues, and negative coverage of a Crane donor has given Democrats an opening, but the incumbent remains clearly favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1703, "margin": -5.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2721, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1924, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2064, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2601, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2994, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2375, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2081, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2582, "margin": -4.5}]}